# LocalRecord is three repos

A change to the app is rarely finished inside this repo. Two other repos consume
it, neither of them in this working tree, and both go stale silently — nothing
fails, the plugin just quietly stops matching the app and the site keeps
describing an older version.

| Repo | Path here | What it is |
|------|-----------|------------|
| App | `~/Work/localrecord` | This one. Rust tray app, Windows + Linux. |
| Landing | `~/Work/localrecord-landing` | Static site → https://localrecord.doublea.engineering |
| Omarchy plugin | `~/.config/omarchy/plugins/doublea.localrecord` | Quickshell bar widget. Its checkout *is* the installed plugin. |

Remotes: `AntoineArt/localrecord`, `AntoineArt/localrecord-landing`,
`AntoineArt/localrecord-omarchy-plugin`.

## What triggers what

Check this before calling any app change done. When a row applies, do that work
in the same session — do not leave it as a follow-up.

| Change in the app | Landing | Plugin |
|---|---|---|
| New or changed user-facing feature | Update the copy that describes it | Surface it in the panel if it is a setting |
| A field added to `state.json` | — | Read it in `Service.qml`, show it if it is user-facing |
| A verb added to the command file | — | Offer it in the panel |
| A field or verb **renamed or removed** | — | **Breaking.** Update the plugin and raise its version floor |
| A setting added to `settings.ini` | Only if documented there | Add a row to the panel — the panel is meant to hold every setting |
| Install steps, requirements, platform support | Update `index.html` | Update the install section of its README |
| Version bump alone | Nothing (the site reads the GitHub API) | Nothing |

The plugin's README names the app version it needs. Raise that floor whenever the
plugin starts relying on something new, and say so in the app's release notes.

## The coupling, concretely

The plugin never imports app code. It talks through two files, and those are the
contract to keep honest:

- **`~/.local/share/localrecord/state.json`** — written by `src/state.rs` on
  every change, read by the plugin's `Service.qml`. Renamed into place; carries
  `pid` so a reader can tell a crashed app from an idle one.
- **`~/.local/share/localrecord/command`** — written by the plugin, read by
  `src/command.rs`. One verb per line; values allowed. Every verb must route
  through the same `App` method the tray menu calls, so the menu, the state file
  and the widget cannot disagree.

Signals (`SIGUSR1` record, `SIGUSR2` auto-levelling) stay the channel for
compositor bindings. Keep them working.

## RELEASE pipeline

When the user says **RELEASE** (or asks to build and release), run every step.

### 1. App

1. Code complete; `cargo test` (two `audio::mixer` tests fail on `main` and have
   for a while — that is the baseline, not your regression) and `cargo build
   --release` clean. `cargo fmt` reformats files nobody touched: revert those, or
   the diff drowns the change.
2. Bump `version` in `Cargo.toml` (patch unless told otherwise) — this also
   touches `Cargo.lock`.
3. Update `README.md` if user-facing behaviour changed.
4. Commit and push to `main`.
5. `git tag -a vX.Y.Z -m "..." && git push origin vX.Y.Z`
6. Wait for `.github/workflows/release.yml` (Windows + Linux build, uploads to a
   GitHub Release).
7. Write the release notes: what changed and why, not a commit list.

### 2. Plugin, if the table above says so

1. Edit in `~/.config/omarchy/plugins/doublea.localrecord` — saving there is
   what the running shell loads, so it is also the test bench.
2. `omarchy plugin validate .` after touching `manifest.json`.
3. Bump `version` in `manifest.json`, update its README, commit, push, and cut a
   GitHub release for the tag.
4. Its testing and portability rules live in that repo's `CLAUDE.md`. Read it —
   in particular, the working tree carries a deliberate local-only edit that must
   not be published.

### 3. Landing, if the table above says so

1. Update `index.html` copy. Download links resolve through `release.js` + the
   GitHub API: never hardcode a version in an href.
2. Commit and push to `main`. **Vercel's git integration deploys production on
   push** — no `vercel --prod` needed.
3. The custom domain does not follow automatically. Point it at the new
   deployment, or the site keeps serving the previous one:
   ```bash
   npx vercel ls localrecord-landing          # newest Production URL
   npx vercel alias set <deployment-url> localrecord.doublea.engineering
   ```
   The CLI may report `Logged out` on its very first `npx` run and work on the
   second — check before concluding the user must log in.

### 4. Verify, with commands not assumptions

```bash
gh release list -R AntoineArt/localrecord --limit 1
gh release view vX.Y.Z --json assets --jq '.assets[].name'   # both .exe and -x86_64-linux
curl -s https://localrecord.doublea.engineering/ | grep -c "<a phrase you just added>"
omarchy-shell doublea.localrecord status                     # the widget answers
```

## Working on this machine

The user runs Hyprland on Omarchy 4 with the Lua config parser, three monitors,
and no microphone plugged in — recordings that capture nothing are expected, not
a bug. Their LocalRecord runs from `~/Work/localrecord/target/release/localrecord`,
so restart that after building. Their shortcut is set through the tray's picker,
which writes `~/.config/hypr/localrecord.lua`.
